                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p93-q5-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Solve ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "f(x)=x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and retain the solution in ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "[3,\\infty)",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The restricted $f$ is increasing, so its intersections with its inverse lie on $y=x$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-6x&=x\\\\x^2-7x&=0\\\\x(x-7)&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $x=0$ or $x=7$. Since $x\\ge3$, only $x=7$ is allowed. With $y=x$, the intersection is $(7,7)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
